## THE HYBRID APPROACH (When Pointers ACTUALLY Help)

There are legitimate cases where pointers are better for memory optimization:

### 1. Polymorphism (Virtual Dispatch)

```cpp
// ✅ POINTERS for polymorphic containers
std::vector<std::unique_ptr<GameObject>> objects;
objects.push_back(std::make_unique<Player>());
objects.push_back(std::make_unique<Enemy>());
objects.push_back(std::make_unique<Item>());

// Each object is different size - can't use vector<GameObject>
// Vector of pointers is the ONLY option
```

### 2. Optional/Nullable Members

```cpp
struct NPC {
    Weapon* weapon;  // nullptr = unarmed
    // 8 bytes (pointer) vs 64+ bytes (full Weapon object)
};

// If only 10% of NPCs have weapons:
// Pointers: 100 NPCs × 8 bytes = 800 bytes
// Full objects: 100 NPCs × 64 bytes = 6400 bytes
// SAVED: 5.6KB!
```

### 3. Object Identity (Avoiding Copies)

```cpp
// ❌ BAD: Copying large objects
void updatePosition(Vector3 pos) {  // Copies 12 bytes
    m_position = pos;  // Another copy
}

// ✅ GOOD: Pointer avoids copies (but reference does too!)
void updatePosition(const Vector3* pos) {  // 8 bytes
    m_position = *pos;  // One copy
}

// ✅ BEST: Reference (same performance, cleaner)
void updatePosition(const Vector3& pos) {  // 8 bytes
    m_position = pos;  // One copy
}
```

